    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d761">

                <pb facs="1034" n="1020" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg1020"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.9331/11–2644: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Atcheson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-11-26T08:00:00+07:00">November 26,
                            1944—8 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 2 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1908. Director of Foreign Office Treaty Department informs me that Chinese draft
                    of commercial treaty is almost ready for presentation. We shall, I feel, have
                    lost the considerable advantages to accrue from putting the Chinese Government
                    on notice as to our desires and expectations, as well as some bargaining
                    advantages, if their draft is presented before ours.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Atcheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
